  "claims": [
    {
      "claim": "Frequentist view defines a random variable as a process whose outcome is unpredictable by definition, representing objective uncertainty.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:34:56\u201300:35:40] 'According to classical view, a random variable is some process whose outcome is unpredictable by definition.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Bayesian view defines a random variable as the result of a completely deterministic process whose outcome is unknown due to subjective ignorance (lack of information).",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:35:40\u201300:36:16] 'according to Bayesian framework, the random variable is the result of a completely deterministic process whose outcome is unknown to us only because we left some information which determines the outcome of this process.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The coin toss outcome is deterministic but unpredictable in practice due to initial conditions and mechanics (e.g., coin's mechanical properties).",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:36:16\u201300:36:52] 'We know that coin abuse the loss of mechanics.' (likely mishearing of 'coin's mechanical properties') and 'even though the initial conditions, how we toss the coin, even though the characteristics of environment, we can, in principle, predict what would be the outcome.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The train arrival time is a random variable that follows a distribution (e.g., empirical density function) that can be updated with new information (e.g., delay announcements).",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:37:23\u201300:38:26] 'I can collect statistics, I can even restore the empirical work and the estimate of a density function of the arrival time.' and 'when the screen shows a 5-minute delay, the distribution updates.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "In the Bayesian view, the randomness of a variable is due to subjective ignorance (lack of information) rather than objective unpredictability.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:38:54\u201300:39:23] 'this is why it's stated here, subjective. So different observers may have different ignorance on the same random variable.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "Both interpretations agree that outcomes are predictable in principle (e.g., physics determines coin toss, traffic conditions determine train arrival).",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:36:53\u201300:37:23] 'We can consider a different example... all physical processes are based on the laws of physics. And the laws of physics give us predictable, give us a verifiable predictions.' and [00:37:53\u201300:38:26] 'there's nothing that is objectively unsolved.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The train's arrival time distribution changes when new information (e.g., delay announcements) is observed, reflecting updated subjective ignorance.",
      "supported": true,
      "evidence_or_problem": "Source: [00:38:26\u201300:38:54] 'I have observed some additional information, the information from the string. So now the distribution of my random variable has changed.'"
    },
    {
      "claim": "The phrase 'coin abuse the loss of mechanics' is a mishearing of 'coin's mechanical properties' (likely a typo in ASR).",
      "supported": false,
      "evidence_or_problem": "This is an unclear point from the notes, not a claim that the ASR supports. The ASR itself may be wrong, but the claim that it's a mishearing is not directly supported by the ASR; it's an interpretation of the ASR's error."
    }
  ],
